    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-026-69896-x",
    "abstract": "Most climate policies are designed under a deterministic Earth system and their climate implications evaluated ex-post. Approaches that incorporate uncertainty ex-ante to anticipate Earth system risks remain underexplored. Here, we derive global climate strategies with an ex-ante approach, employing an integrated assessment framework that embeds estimates of physical uncertainty obtained through Bayesian fusion of Earth system models' and observations' data. These ex-ante strategies mitigate risks in the Earth system through precautionary measures unseen with the ex-post approach, in cost-benefit analysis and cost-effective implementations of various Earth system targets. Net-zero CO<sub>2</sub> emissions must typically be reached a decade earlier, which can require up to a doubling of the near-term carbon price. Importantly, sustained and possibly century-long net-negative emissions must be planned for, albeit not to overshoot targets as in traditional scenarios but to mitigate long-term Earth system risks. This heightens the challenge faced by humanity to build a safe future within Earth system boundaries.",
    "field": "Integrated assessment of climate change",
    "problem": "Most climate policies are designed using an \"ex-post\" approach where uncertainty is dealt with after strategies are created, while \"ex-ante\" approaches that incorporate physical uncertainty into the initial design to anticipate and mitigate Earth system risks remain underexplored.",
    "method": "We propose an ex-ante approach using an integrated assessment framework, which embeds physical uncertainty through Bayesian fusion of Earth system models' and observations' data, to derive global climate strategies that anticipate and mitigate long-term Earth system risks.",
    "task": "The task is to derive and assess global climate strategies using an ex-ante approach that incorporates physical uncertainty into an integrated assessment framework to anticipate and mitigate Earth system risks, comparing them to traditional ex-post strategies.",
